vmx development

Build inside generated9:

	cd /sys/src/9/pc64
	mk
	cd /sys/src/cmd/vmx
	mk install
	mk test

Boot the new kernel before using the monitor. In the checkout environment:

	./boot/qemu -m 4G -smp 4 -kernel sys/src/9/pc64/9pc64

The outer host needs KVM with nested Intel VMX enabled. Guest options and
limits are in vmx(1); the kernel interface is in vmx(3). boot/VOID.md gives
a complete Linux desktop setup with graphics and audio.

Execution

Each virtual CPU has a #X instance and local APIC; all share guest memory.
Kernel processes execute CPUs concurrently. One monitor loop handles exits,
devices and worker notifications. Only that loop selects the current CPU or
uses the register cache. Register snapshots are decoded on demand and
invalidated on resume. HLT/MSR exit fields avoid extra register reads;
older kernels use the register-file fallback.

xAPIC and x2APIC share interrupt and timer state. Intel MP 1.4 tables describe
CPUs and interrupt routes. ACPI, SMI delivery and APIC relocation are absent;
the debugger selects CPU 0. The single-CPU default retains the PIC path.

An IPI wakes a CPU handling a VM command. Before guest reentry, the kernel
yields to runnable processes so the monitor can run. When CPUs are shared,
PAUSE exits yield to other processes, then execute PAUSE under a monitor trap.
VPID invalidation and guest entry cannot be interrupted by a context switch.

Guest TSC uses the host counter plus a per-CPU offset. TSC_ADJUST cancels
host software adjustments; hardware counters must be synchronized.
Without it, the host's adjusted counters must already be synchronized.
Guest writes affect only the virtual offset and survive INIT.
The monitor advertises invariant TSC only when the host provides it;
boot/qemu exposes it through KVM's host,migratable=off CPU model.

Removing ctl releases the CPU and its mappings immediately, including with
ORCLOSE. Old descriptors cannot reach a reused slot. Inspect a stopped CPU
before removing it; the last removed CPU is no longer retained for debugging.

Graphics

-g exposes a modern VirtIO GPU with VirGL capability set 1. Guest rendering
contexts map to gpu(3) contexts; allocation contexts keep resources alive
independently. The bridge translates resource IDs; object names, shaders
and subcontexts pass through. Disk and network retain legacy VirtIO transport.

Query backing is synchronized in both directions so guest resets precede
reuse. Results are published before their ready flags. Queue workers
serialize GPU operations and complete fences after host completion;
interrupts go through the monitor loop.

There are at most 4096 guest resources, 64 MiB backing per resource,
32 host contexts and 256 MiB backing per context. Allocation contexts count
toward the limit. Scatter lists and readback use copies; partial readback
preserves bytes outside the transfer box. GPU updates wake presentation;
legacy framebuffers poll every 20 ms. There is no video decoding, Vulkan,
resource blob transport or GPU passthrough. See gpu(3) and UPSTREAM.gpu.

Audio

-a exposes one VirtIO Sound stream: stereo S16LE PCM at 44100 Hz.
The backend opens at START and closes at RELEASE/reset. The guest mixes
and resamples; audio/mixfs can share the host output. There is no input stream.

One worker handles control and playback. Host writes pace playback;
stat's length supplies queued output bytes. Control takes priority between
periods. RELEASE publishes pending completions before its response;
notification holds no locks or live buffers. Periods are at most 64 KiB,
guest buffers at most 1 MiB. The backend must provide bounded write
backpressure as audio(3) does; a blocked pipe can delay control and reset.

Tests

mk test needs no VMX hardware. It checks instruction decoding, registers,
APICs, timers, VirtIO queues and sound, including reset and failure paths.

Device tests require VMX on the generated9 host:

	mk test/6.device
	test/6.device

Inside an amd64 generated9 guest, run the process test with its expected
CPU count:

	cd /sys/src/cmd/vmx
	mk test/6.plan9
	test/6.plan9 2

The first checks TSC, exits, stepping and CPU/mapping lifetime. The second
checks CPU count, pinned processes, shared atomics, floating point and time.

From the Linux host, test/run builds the monitor and boots nested Linux:

	sys/src/cmd/vmx/test/run /boot/vmlinuz-VERSION --modules VERSION

It needs the rebuilt generated9 kernel, nested Intel VMX, Python 3 and a
static Linux C toolchain. The bzImage needs SMP, Intel MP tables, initramfs,
devtmpfs, x2APIC, CPU hotplug, Magic SysRq and a serial console. VirtIO PCI
and block support must be built in or supplied as matching host modules;
omit --modules for built-ins. Zstd modules require Python 3.14.

--cpus selects guest counts (default 1 2 4 8); --host-cpus selects the outer
count (default 4). --host-cpus 2 --cpus 4 exercises oversubscription.
--xapic tests nox2apic; --timeout sets seconds per guest (default 900).

Tests cover CPU count, atomics, floating point, timers, memory protection,
fork isolation, concurrent disk I/O, hotplug and NMI delivery. SMP must use
TSC without clocksource overrides and keep time across hotplug. The PIC
path still lacks PIT mode 4 for one-shot timer events. Logs and disposable
images remain under usr/glenda/tmp/vmx-smp-*; Linux is not downloaded.

Validated under amd64 nested KVM with Linux 6.18.56: 1/2/4/8 CPUs on four
host CPUs, four on two, and the four-CPU xAPIC fallback. Native generated9
guests passed with two and four CPUs on two host CPUs. The 64-CPU limit is
not a tested configuration. The 386 sources compile; runtime tests used amd64.

For the VirGL query regression, build on Linux with X11/GL headers and
run under Xorg/Mesa in the nested guest:

	cc -O2 -o virgl test/virgl.c -lGL -lX11
	./virgl

It reuses a timestamp query over 300 frames, requiring increasing results.
It needs GL_ARB_timer_query or OpenGL 3.3. See boot/VOID.md for the outer
renderer polling defect this catches.

References

Intel SDM: instruction encoding, WRMSR, initialization, APICs, VMX events,
EPT/VPID, IA32_TSC_ADJUST, PAUSE exiting and monitor trap flag.
https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/technical/intel-sdm.html

Intel x2APIC Specification, 318148-004 (March 2010), sections 2.3, 2.4, 2.7.
https://courses.cs.washington.edu/courses/cse451/21wi/readings/x2apic.pdf
Intel MultiProcessor Specification 1.4: tables and interrupt assignments.

Linux 6.18: check_system_tsc_reliable and calibrate_delay_is_known.
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.18/arch/x86/kernel/tsc.c

VirtIO 1.2: PCI transport (4.1), GPU (5.7) and sound (5.14).
https://docs.oasis-open.org/virtio/virtio/v1.2/virtio-v1.2.html

See CREDITS.md and UPSTREAM.gpu for provenance and licenses.
